hi my name's alan i'm a postdoc in  jordan rush lab in oxford university  and today i'm going to talk about a new  model to probe the pathway of central  and centrosome assembly  that we've termed the saps or sussex and  the two particles  so in jordan's lab we work on centrals  and centrosomes  centrioles are these cylindrical shaped  organelles  which are found in most eukaryotic cells  and they form two important structures  the first of these are the cilium this  is when the centroid migrates to the  plasma membrane and forms  protrusions which can either be motile  or non-motile  when non-motile they're often a  signaling center for the cell  the second structure that the central  can form are the centrosomes  which are the major microtubule  organizing centers of the cell  so why is central's interesting to study  well when you have central dysfunction  this can result in malfunctioning  psyllium  which ends up with various pathologies  such as microcephaly primary dwarfism or  various ciliopathies  meanwhile if centrosomes aren't formed  properly this can result in problems  with spindle formation  resulting in cell polarity cell motility  or spindle alignment issues and as a  result  centrosomes are often associated with  multiple cancers  in our lab we're interested in how  centrosomes are formed  now remember centrosome is a centriole  surrounded by  the so-called pericentronal material a  scaffold of proteins  required for the nucleation of  microtubules  over many years both genetic and  biochemical studies have identified the  key components required for both central  assembly  and pcm formation as you can see  there's a very there's only a very few  proteins required  five for central assembly asterisk plk  for  sus6 and a2 and sus4 and even fewer for  pcm assembly  asterisks spot-2 polo and cnn  all of these components are conserved in  most high eukaryotes and especially  between humans and drosophila  in our lab we use the embryo of the  fruit fly drosophila melanogaster as our  model  for uh studying centriole and centrosome  behavior  as you can see from this movie the  centroids and centrosomes in these  embryos  divide uh very quickly and all in  synchrony  this enables um very simple  quantification of the events  uh going on there is however a problem  with studying  centriole and centrosome formation in  drosophila  and that is that  mutation in any of the five core central  assembly factors  results in the formation of no central  whatsoever  therefore it's very hard to tease apart  the interaction  between the proteins of central assembly  and also it doesn't help with studying  the interaction between  central uh the central and the pcm that  assembles around it  so in today's talk i'm going to discuss  how we've developed a model to study  central and centrosome behavior in the  absence of any of these core central  assembly factors  that model is the saps or sus6 and  another two particles  then i'm going to talk about why we  think they're such a good model for the  for these studies  and then give one brief example of how  we've used the saps to probe centrosome  protein behavior in the absence of one  of these key  core central assembly factors  so the origins of the sap project come  from this publication from our lab about  10 years ago  in this paper the authors were mildly  over expressing  various centrosome duplication factors  in unfertilized eggs now it's important  to state here that  females from drosophila will lay  unfertilized eggs  which don't have any centrioles in them  normally  when an egg is fertilized centrol is  introduced by the male sperm  so these unfertilized eggs normally  don't contain any centroids at all  so the authors found that when you  overexpressed anna 2 to moderate levels  the unfertilized eggs didn't contain any  structures  and similarly when sus6 was also  moderately overexpressed  nothing was seen within the egg however  when both  sus6 and ana2 were co-overexpressed at  moderate levels  particles started to appear in the  cytoplasm of these eggs  these were termed the sus6 another  particles or saps this would suggest  that sussex and anna2 can co-assemble  into higher order structures  in this cytoplasm but what's remarkable  about these assemblies  is when we injected them into cycling  wild-type embryos so what i'm showing  you here is a movie  of a wild type embryo expressing a  microtubule marker in red  with this the saps injected in labelled  in green  and what you can see here is that their  saps  uh organized robust asterisk and  microtubules  whose dynamics appear to cycle basically  in synchrony with the  astral microtubules that are being  organized from the endogenous  centrosomes  so this movie clearly showed that sapps  could organize microtubules  but what about other centriole and pcm  components  when these two panels you can see a  comparison between  saps and centrioles staying for various  central  and centrosome markers i hope you can  appreciate that saps can recruit  robust levels of central proteins  sus4 and atlas as well as pcm components  spud 2 and cnn in a similar way to  that as it's seen in centrioles  so we went on to analyze a bit more  precisely how various central components  were being recruited to the saps  and compare them to how they're  recruited to endogenous centrioles  what i'm showing you here in the top  panel is how sas4 is recruited to the  centrioles  and you can see from the quantification  in the graph that sas4 has a slightly  broader slightly broader distribution  compared to the core central marker  when we now compare this to how sas4 is  recruited to saps  and measure the distance from the edge  of the sap wall  and see where sas4 is localized you can  see it has a very similar distribution  to uh how sas4 is recruited into  endogenous centrioles  we then went on to do a similar analysis  for other components here i'm showing  you  atlas being recruited to centrioles and  to saps  and you can see from the quantification  in the graph that atlas recruited to  saps  has very similar distribution to how  acilis is recruited to centrals  this uh demonstrates that actually the  organization of proteins is remarkably  similar  in both saps and centrioles so it seems  that saps able to organize microtubules  and pcm in a very similar fashion to  that scene endogenous centrosomes  so we're interested to see how the pcm  proteins  cnn and spa2 behaved  at saps compared to centrosomes  these two proteins are phosphorylated  when they're  localized to centrosomes whereas in the  cytosol they're not phosphorylated  if we then look at look to see how these  proteins  behave on the saps we can see that  they're both phosphorylated  compared to their proteins similar  proteins  in cytosol so we can say the pcn  proteins on saps have they're very  have very similar post-translational  modifications  compared to endogenous centrosomes the  crystal structure of both  sus6 and parts of hana 2 are already  known  so we're interested to see whether the  interactions between these two proteins  that occur in endogenous centrioles to  form the beautiful  nine-fold radial symmetric cartwheel  structure  are also present in the formation of  saps the first the residues that we  tried  to mutate was that in the head group of  sus6  now sus6 oligomerizes to form the  cartwheel structure  and when certain residues within the  head groups are mutated  this oligomerization can no longer  happen  and as a result the cartwheel doesn't  form and centrioles don't form  when we overexpress this form of sus6 to  moderate levels along with  ana2 at similar moderate levels we saw  that no  saps could form we also mutated  some of the domains in anna 2 in a  similar way  so ana2 has several domains which are  required for  its polygonalization the coil coil  domains  and for its interaction with sus6 the  so-called stand domain  when the stand domain was was mutated  so such that it no longer interacts with  sus6  and in a wild type situation this  prevents the formation  of the cartwheel and therefore central  formation  when we express this form to moderate  levels in  in our sap model system we could see  that saps no longer formed so the stand  domain in anna 2 is essential for sap  formation  in a similar manner the coil coil domain  of  anna 2 is required for its  oligomerization and this oligomerization  is required for its interaction with  sus6 and the formation  of an endogenous carpal structure in  centrioles  when this was expressed moderate levels  in our sap system  saps no longer formed so these  experiments suggest  that slaps are not simply non-specific  aggregates of proteins  but remarkably require the same  protein protein interactions that  are normally occurring in endogenous  centrioles  but clearly saps aren't centrioles  you can see from this graph this is  quantification of the area  of the central compared to a sap and  saps have a  much broader range distribution of their  their size and saps are much larger than  central's and as a result the total  sussex intensity within  saps is much greater than you can see  within  central's however  when we looked by em  at saps we could see no obvious uh  structure  so there was there wasn't this beautiful  ninefold radial symmetric  cartwheel structure that you can see  within endogenous centrioles  but this was our advantage because  they aren't central they can form even  in the absence  of some of the core centriole proteins  so just to recap what i've told you so  far  saps form when sussex and ana2 are  co-overexpressed at moderate levels  this suggests they have a remarkable  property of um  self organization into into  large co assemblies sap formation  requires many of the same protein  protein interactions that are occurring  in endogenous centrioles  and saps recruit central and centrosome  proteins in a similar fashion  to that seen in endogenous centrosomes  in fact sap show many similar behaviors  to centrioles  in their ability to recruit pcm and then  form microtubule asters so i'm just  going to change gear a tiny bit  and talk a bit about the developmental  biology of  flies which lack centrioles  so as i've mentioned already central is  important in forming two structures  within most cells  those are the psyllium and the  centrosome  now when any mutants in the core  central assembly factors is made in  flies  this results obviously in the lack of  centrioles  but as a result these flyers also lack  both the psyllium and centrosome however  remarkably  you can still get adult flies that lack  centrioles  as was published a few years ago from  our lab  these adult flies without centrioles are  remarkably healthy  in all but one aspect and that is that  as they lack centrioles they also  lack cilia in their mechanocentric  organs  this means that they are uncoordinated  as you can see in this movie  they can't stand they can't fly they  can't feed themselves they have no  perception of the world around them  so this presents us with somewhat of a  problem if we want to study the eggs  from these females  as uncoordinated mutants don't tend to  lay many eggs  we overcame this problem by over  expressing using the uas gal4  system through  central duplication factor simply in the  canary sensory cells of the females  these females are otherwise mutant for  the  central assembly factor and therefore  they lay eggs  which totally lack the central protein  of interest  we can then assay whether saps can form  even in the absence  of the other core centriole proteins  one of the core central assembly factors  we were particularly interested in  was asthelus now as you can see from  this cartoon atlas has  somewhat of a dual role both in central  assembly  but also in pcm formation  as uh mutants in athelist don't have any  centrioles  it's very hard to assess the role of  athletes  in pcm assembly around the centrals  because they're no longer present  in uh a massless mutants  this is where the sap model came comes  in very  handy because as you can see from this  graph  about half of the embryos from an asylus  mutant  contain saps  compared to wild type the efficiency of  sat formation and astute's mutant is  decreased  but they're still present so excitingly  saps can form  even in the absence of atlas  this means we now have an assay where we  can assess the role of asylus in pcm  assembly  even in the absence of endogenous  centrioles  we also had a couple of tools we could  use these are two  asless mutants one asylus one  uh is a short truncation of atlas  where some truncated protein is still  produced  and this seems to act as a hypermorphos  where centrioles are present but pcm  formation is inhibited  the second mutant is a much stronger  mutant asylus mcd  this lacks centrioles and is completely  uncoordinated  but its role in pcm is somewhat unclear  we therefore express sussex and anatou  to form saps in both  the hypomorphic asylus one mutant  and an asylus null mutant and then  stained for the presence of spud2 and  cnn  for the presence of pcm components  as expected you can see in the graph  here  the percentage of saps in an atlas one  mutant  staining for cnn orthospa 2 was  basically  zero however unexpectedly  in the atlas null mutant the percentage  of saps  containing cnn and spud 2  was significantly above zero almost  fifty percent in the case of cnn  this suggests that the presence of the  truncated protein that's present in  anacelus  one mutant prevents pcm recruitment  and that in the total absence of  asterisks  um pcm can  actually form the question was how is  this  pcm recruited in the total absence of  atlas  to quickly address this issue we uh  expressed  sussex and anna ii to form saps in a  double  spa ii asterisk mutant  and then stained for the presence of  spud 2 or cnn  you can now see in this double mutant  cnn  is no longer present this suggests  that in the absence of athletes there is  an uh asylus independent backup pathway  for pcm formation  and that this backup path rate requires  spud 2  in order to recruit the pcm it seems  however that this backup pathway  appears to be much less efficient  compared to the main pcm recruitment  pathway which is asthelus dependent  so if we look at either spud2 or cnn  recruitment in wild-type saps  the intensities of these proteins  recruited is much greater  compared to the pcm recruited in the  absence  of atlas so if we look at our essential  assembly and pcm assembly pathway  this remains as was described with atlas  being  the main component for pcm assembly  formation  however in the absence of atlas there is  this second backup pathway  where something in the centriole is  required  to recruit the pcm via spud 2  in a much less efficient way so just to  summarize this quick  example of how we've used saps as a tool  to probe  central and centrosome proteins in the  absence of centrioles  saps form in the absence of atolus and  pcm can form around these saps  even in the total absence of athletes  this atlas independent pathway requires  spa  two but actually it's much less  efficient than the standard  asterisk dependent pathway for pcm  assembly  this work is part of uh part of a  publication that was just published  last month so if you want to read more  about the saps  i direct you to this paper in a journal  of cell science  so that just leaves me to thank you very  much for your attention  i'd also like to thank the raph lab in  particular jordan raff  but also cat percent and lisa garteman  for their work on the saps

